"(PDF) Everyday Climates: Household Archaeologies and the Politics of Scale (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/392725253_Everyday_Climates_Household_Archaeologies_and_the_Politics_of_Scale)\nciteturn11search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.3 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # Everyday Climates: Household Archaeologies and the Politics of Scale ... In this paper I highlight how households themselves were critical sites of environmental construction, experience and history-making through a selection of examples of archeological work from the Mediterranean. ... Citation: Kearns, C. ... Copyright: © 2025 by the author. ... Keywords: scale; household archeology; Mediterranean; everyday; climate ... Heritage 2025,8, 227 https://doi.org/10.3390/heritage8060227 ... Others confront households as a cultural trait, social historical practice or category of ... other hand by political and economic forces extending beyond the local community?” ... instigate research opportunities for smaller-scale archaeologies of climate: the storage of ... consumption and discard and domestic work highlights the interpretive potential of house- ... twenty-ﬁrst century attempts to refocus conversations on environmental policy and regula-\nArticle PDF Available\n\n# Everyday Climates: Household Archaeologies and the Politics of Scale\n\n  * June 2025\n  * Heritage 8(6):227\n\nDOI:10.3390/heritage8060227\n\n  * License\n  * CC BY 4.0\n\nAuthors:\n\nCatherine Kearns\n\nCatherine Kearns\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nImage\n\nDownload full-text PDFRead full-text\n\n## Abstract and Figures\n\nThe small scale is recognized as a necessary rebuttal to macroscalar narratives of climate–society relationships in the past, and archeologists and historians have increasingly turned to advocating smaller and shorter scales of analysis and interpretation, from “microclimates” to interannual droughts and single settlement histories. Such provocations rightly caution against the dangers of oversimplification and determinism in recent planetary or Earth-systems approaches to human history, as well as push scholars to acknowledge human-scale experiences: weather, seasonality, landscape change. \nImage: ResearchGate Logo\n\nDiscover the world's research\n\n  * 25+ million members\n  * 160+ million publication pages\n  * 2.3+ billion citations\nJoin for free\n\nPublisher Full-text 1\n\nAccess to this full-text is provided by MDPI.\n\nLearn more\n\nContent available from Heritage\n\nThis content is subject to copyright.\n\nAcademic Editors: Sharon Steadman\n\nand John Frederick Haldon\n\nReceived: 15 April 2025\n\nRevised: 30 May 2025\n\nAccepted: 10 June 2025\n\nPublished: 14 June 2025\n\nCitation: Kearns, C. Everyday\n\nClimates: Household Archaeologies\n\nand the Politics of Scale. Heritage 2025,\n\n8, 227. https://doi.org/10.3390/\n\nheritage8060227\n\nCopyright: © 2025 by the author.\n\nLicensee MDPI, Basel, Switzerland.\n\nThis article is an open access article\n\ndistributed under the terms and\n\nconditions of the Creative Commons\n\nAttribution (CC BY) license\n\n(https://creativecommons.org/\n\nlicenses/by/4.0/).\n\nArticle\n\nEveryday Climates: Household Archaeologies and the Politics\n\nof Scale\n\nCatherine Kearns\n\nDepartment of Classics, University of Chicago, Chicago, IL 60637, USA; cmkearns@uchicago.edu\n\nAbstract: The small scale is recognized as a necessary rebuttal to macroscalar narratives\n\nof climate–society relationships in the past, and archeologists and historians have increas-\n\ningly turned to advocating smaller and shorter scales of analysis and interpretation, from\n\n“microclimates” to interannual droughts and single settlement histories. Such provoca-\n\ntions rightly caution against the dangers of oversimpliﬁcation and determinism in recent\n\nplanetary or Earth-systems approaches to human history, as well as push scholars to ac-\n\nknowledge human-scale experiences: weather, seasonality, landscape change. When it\n\ncomes to smaller-scale remains, however, like those of household practices, we often con-\n\nsider them data or proxies that validate larger-scale arguments about societal persistence\n\nor economic vulnerability. Yet the material and ideational ways that people in premod-\n\nern worlds made sense of their surroundings, especially via gendered and class-based\n\nrhythms of production and consumption, were deeply entwined in the politics of everyday\n\nhousehold life. What would a household archeology of climate entail? In this paper I\n\nhighlight how households themselves were critical sites of environmental construction,\n\nexperience and history-making through a selection of examples of archeological work\n\nfrom the Mediterranean. I argue that archeologists can critically rethink themes of persis-\n\ntence and adaptation by taking seriously the scalar constructions and varied politics of\n\ndomestic life.\n\nKeywords: scale; household archeology; Mediterranean; everyday; climate\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFuture directions for Anthropocene research and its applications | Nature Reviews Earth & Environment (https://www.nature.com/articles/s43017-026-00820-z)\nciteturn11search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: 4 days ago; Nature Reviews Earth & Environment volume 7, pages 633–646 (2026) ...   * Dedicated research and policy institutions reflect how the Anthropocene concept has already influenced particular fields, including politics, economics, history and education, and helped shift environmental science towards Earth system thinking. ... Earth System Science, Anthropocene historiography, and three forms of human agency. ... Planetary pictures: historicizing environmental and climate sciences in the Anthropocene. ... Griffith Review https://www.griffithreview.com/articles/planet-is-alive-radical-history/ (2019).\n\n  * Perspective\n  * Published: 08 September 2026\n\n# Future directions for Anthropocene research and its applications\n\nNature Reviews Earth & Environment volume 7, pages 633–646 (2026)\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe term ‘Anthropocene’ was coined in recognition that human activities have ended the relatively stable environment of the Holocene epoch. However, its widespread adoption across the humanities, arts and sciences has led to diverse and sometimes incompatible definitions. In this Perspective, we argue for a stabilized definition of the Anthropocene epoch to help address contemporary environmental challenges, and explore various applications of the Anthropocene across disciplines. The Anthropocene epoch, with a proposed start point in 1952, underscores that human-driven global-scale environmental disruption differs from earlier human impacts of the Holocene, which did not cause such profound destabilization. This clear distinction enables quantitative and qualitative comparisons between the Holocene and Anthropocene epochs, as already utilized, for instance, within the planetary boundaries framework. As the Anthropocene Earth system transformation is systemic, political responses also need be systemic, rather than ad hoc. A unified Anthropocene epoch would help facilitate systematic, actionable climate and environmental policies. By contrast, alternative, extended interpretations of the Anthropocene are used to reflect accumulated human environmental impacts over many millennia. These broader interpretations portray the totality of anthropogenic change but obscure the quantitatively established departure from Holocene conditions. Applying a consistent Anthropocene epoch definition will improve its utility to science, scholarship and policy.\n\n## Key points\n\n  * Dedicated research and policy institutions reflect how the Anthropocene concept has already influenced particular fields, including politics, economics, history and education, and helped shift environmental science towards Earth system thinking.\n\n  * Human-driven environmental changes have long had political importance, but since the 1950s the global systemic scale and accelerating speed of Earth system changes have acquired societal and political relevance of an entirely new order and magnitude.\n\n  * The Anthropocene is best and most clearly understood in its original meaning, as representing an epoch characterized by the human-driven departure from the 12-millennia-long, relatively stable planetary conditions of the Holocene epoch. Its base is most optimally placed at 1952, corresponding to the mid-twentieth-century onset of the Great Acceleration.\n\n  * Other uses of the term Anthropocene, which encompass all human impacts over tens of thousands of years, mask the dramatic changes to the Earth system that began in the mid-twentieth century and cannot be defined in chronostratigraphic terms, and therefore represent a concept distinct from the Anthropocene epoch.\n\n  * The central purpose of a clear, formalized definition is to facilitate quantitative and qualitative comparisons between the stable Holocene and increasingly unstable Anthropocene, and to aid more unified communication across the Earth sciences, social sciences, humanities, society and political and legal fora.\n\nFig. 1: The state of the Earth system in the Anthropocene.\n\nImage\n\nFig. 2: Anthropocene as part of the Quaternary.\n\nImage\n\nFig. 3: The Great Acceleration graphs.\n\nImage\n\n## References\n\n  1. Syvitski, J. et al. Extraordinary human energy consumption and resultant geological impacts beginning around 1950 CE initiated the proposed Anthropocene Epoch. Commun. Earth Environ. 1, 32 (2020).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Anthropocene debate | Nature Reviews Earth & Environment (https://www.nature.com/articles/s43017-026-00829-4)\nciteturn11search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: yesterday; Nature Reviews Earth & Environment volume 7, pages 563–564 (2026) ... The Anthropocene has become a highly influential and popular concept describing humans as a planetary force reshaping the Earth system. ... Human-driven environmental change has no clear precedent in geological history; therefore, any future Anthropocene epoch might need to possess a character as unique as the phenomenon it seeks to describe. ... Nat Rev Earth Environ 7, 563–564 (2026). https://doi.org/10.1038/s43017-026-00829-4\n\nThe Anthropocene debate\n\n  * Editorial\n  * Published: 08 September 2026\n\n# The Anthropocene debate\n\nNature Reviews Earth & Environment volume 7, pages 563–564 (2026)\n\nIn 2024, geologists rejected the Anthropocene as a new epoch amid dispute over the definition of the term. This Focus issue and online Collection bring together opinion pieces on the renewed Anthropocene debate.\n\n### Subjects\n\n  * Environmental impact\n  * Scientific community\n  * Stratigraphy\n\nHuman activities are transforming climate, ecosystems, landscapes and biogeochemical cycles at unprecedented scale and speed. These anthropogenic perturbations of the Earth system pose profound risks, including the potential for irreversible environmental change, biodiversity loss, resource depletion, and the transgression of planetary boundaries. Yet, despite broad agreement that humanity has become a dominant force shaping the Earth system, substantial debate remains over how this new planetary condition should be defined in geological time.\n\nThe Anthropocene has become a highly influential and popular concept describing humans as a planetary force reshaping the Earth system. However, despite its widespread use across the natural sciences, social sciences, and humanities, disagreement remains over its meaning. This Focus issue brings together opinions from different sides of the debate: whether the Anthropocene is best interpreted as a formal geological epoch succeeding the Holocene (see the Perspectives by Waters et al. and Zalasiewicz et al.), or as a broader geological event encompassing the cumulative impacts of humans on the Earth system (see the Comments by Zhou et al. and Merritts and Walter). An accompanying Collection highlights articles from across the Nature Portfolio that have covered the Anthropocene debate and helped shape the evolution of the term.\n\nThe debate surrounding the Anthropocene intensified in 2024, following the rejection of the Anthropocene Working Group’s proposal to formalize an Anthropocene epoch with a proposed start date of 1952. However, any future renewed proposal would need to satisfy formal chronostratigraphic criteria while preserving the wider scientific and societal value that made the Anthropocene such an influential concept. Human-driven environmental change has no clear precedent in geological history; therefore, any future Anthropocene epoch might need to possess a character as unique as the phenomenon it seeks to describe.\n\nUltimately, both epoch and event concepts provide frameworks to understand humanity’s transformative role in Earth history and emphasize the need for planetary stewardship to support a sustainable future. Regardless of the Anthropocene’s meaning or stratigraphic status, the essential task for all Earth and environmental scientists remains to urgently help society confront the issues of climate change, pollution and biodiversity loss.\n\n## References\n\n  1. Crutzen, P. J. & Stoermer, E. F. The Anthropocene. Glob. Change Newsl. 41, 17–18 (2000).\n\n  2. Gibbard, P. L. et al. A practical solution: the Anthropocene is a geological event, not a formal epoch. Episodes 45, 349–357 (2022).\n\n  3. Rockström, J. et al. Planetary boundaries guide humanity’s future on Earth. Nat Rev Earth Environ. 5, 773–788 (2024).\n\n  4. Rull, V. The Anthropocene: proposal, rejection, consequences, alternatives, and a lesson. J. Quat. Sci. 41, 661–665 (2026).\n\n## Additional information\n\n### Related links\n\nGeologists reject the Anthropocene as Earth’s new epoch: https://doi.org/10.1038/d41586-024-00675-8\n\nIUGS-ICS Anthropocene statement: https://www.iugs.org/the-anthropocene-iugs-ics-statement/\n\n## About this article\n\n### Cite this article\n\nThe Anthropocene debate. Nat Rev Earth Environ 7, 563–564 (2026). https://doi.org/10.1038/s43017-026-00829-4\n\n  * Published: 08 September 2026\n\n  * Version of record: 08 September 2026\n\n  * Issue date: September 2026\n\n  * DOI (Digital Object Identifier): https://doi.org/10.1038/s43017-026-00829-4--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEarth System Science, Anthropocene Historiography, and Three Forms of Human Agency | Isis: Vol 113, No 2 (https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/10.1086/719647)\nciteturn11search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.3 years ago; Crawled: 6 days ago; This Anthropocene Earth System was first visualized in 1986 in the Bretherton diagram of our planet’s workings. ... Taking ESS seriously in historical interpretations—not only in the history of science but across environmental humanities of all kinds—is the precondition for developing an Anthropocene historiography distinct from existing historical approaches: distinct, and much more radical.Because ESS sees humanity as integral to the current planetary transformation, it constructs a new relationship between the sciences and the historically minded humanities, leading to what we call “a science-induced Anthropocene historiography” and also, we hope, to “a humanities-induced Anthropocene science.” ... ^{5} What is new about Anthropocene history—and Anthropocene history of science—is that all three forms of agency must now be kept in view by scientists and historians alike.\n\nWhat crucially distinguishes Anthropocene history from environmental history is this new type of systemic human agency. Like all creatures, human beings have always altered their environments in various ways. For the first time ever, in producing the Anthropocene, human beings collectively have altered the Earth System. Environmental history represents a crucial step in moving away from the modern distinction between human and natural history. Its emergence as a knowledge formation is inseparable from the mid-twentieth-century emergence of the notion of the environment as distinct from nature.^{28} As Sverker Sörlin and Nina Wormbs argue, humans “make” environments, they turn “nature” into “environment” through “environing technologies.”^{29} Environmental history is premised on this relatively new idea of conceiving the environment as inseparable from human intervention. But the idea of Earth System agency was not conceivable within the framework of environmental thinking and environmental history—it emerges only recently, outside environmental history, through an encounter with ESS.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n‘Welcome to the Anthropocene’: Public Environmental History | Public History Review (https://epress.lib.uts.edu.au/journals/index.php/phrj/article/view/8982)\nciteturn11search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.2 years ago; Crawled: 3 days ago; The geological concept ‘the Anthropocene’ circulates widely outside Earth System Science – in museum communities, public culture, social sciences, and amongst social science and humanities scholars, artists, activists and public historians too. ... This article discusses the concept of the Anthropocene within public environmental history’s historiography and brings in Dipesh Chakrabarty’s influential discussions from the academic field of history and theory about the non-synchronous temporalities geological time and historical time.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDISCIPLINING THE ANTHROPOCENE - Hesketh - 2022 - History and Theory - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/hith.12267)\nciteturn11search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Wh