ble of producing change in human history, but specific focus on animals has been very rare. ... The sub-discipline maintains similarities with various movements in African historiography aimed at recognising agency, and is sometimes incorporated into environmental histories.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEarly anthropocene (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Early_anthropocene)\nciteturn11search18 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 9 months ago; In contrast, a 2025 review by environmental historian Jeremy Davies argued that the Anthropocene should still be formally recognized as a distinct epoch, emphasizing that Earth-system changes since the mid-twentieth century mark a clear departure from Holocene conditions. ... Humanities scholar Elizabeth DeLoughrey has posited that while the Early Anthropocene Hypothesis \"traces out an eight-thousand-year history of deforestation,\" it \"never contextualizes the histories of human violence.Consequently, in explaining those eras in which CO2 did not rise due to a significant drop in the production of agriculture caused by death, [Ruddiman] likens the plague in Medieval Europe to the decimation of 90 percent of the Indigenous peoples of the Americas, referring to it simply as a 'pandemic' rather than genocide.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAnthropocene (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthropocene)\nciteturn11search19 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; Potawatomi philosopher Kyle Powys Whyte has further argued that the Anthropocene has been apparent to Indigenous peoples in the Americas since the inception of colonialism because of \"colonialism's role in environmental change.\" ... Historians have actively engaged the Anthropocene.In 2000, the same year that Paul Crutzen coined the term, world historian John McNeill published Something New Under the Sun, tracing the rise of human societies' unprecedented impact on the planet in the twentieth century.In 2001, historian of science Naomi Oreskes revealed the systematic efforts to undermine trust in climate change science and went on to detail the corporate interests delaying action on the environmental challenge.Both McNeill and Oreskes became members of the Anthropocene Working Group because of their work correlating human activities and planetary transformation. ... Oxford Literary Review.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nResidual Governance (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Residual_Governance)\nciteturn11search20 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; Subject: Environmental history, mining in South Africa, racial capitalism, Anthropocene ... Residual Governance: How South Africa Foretells Planetary Futures is a 2023 book by historian and anthropologist Gabrielle Hecht that traces the environmental and social impacts of gold and uranium mining in South Africa's Witwatersrand region.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEnvironmental history of the United States (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Environmental_history_of_the_United_States)\nciteturn11search21 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 7 months ago; Pacific Historical Review 47.4 (1978): 507–564.online; Covers 1) irrigation, 1878–1902, 2) reclamation service, 3) agricultural settlement, 1902–28, 4) engineering 1887–1953, 5) Department of Agriculture, 1898–1938, 6) historians, 1898–1978, and 7) challenges to Bureau ... A Companion to American Environmental History (2010), 696pp; 33 essays by scholars that emphasize the historiography; online\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n'Just as we thought we were finally alone in an artificial world, the Earth responds to us with force' (https://www.lemonde.fr/en/opinion/article/2026/09/16/just-as-we-thought-we-were-finally-alone-in-an-artificial-world-the-earth-responds-to-us-with-force_6757574_23.html)\nciteturn11news22 [wordlim: 100] Published: 2 days ago; This rupture with our nourishing environment is unprecedented in human history. ... Heatwaves, wildfires, and more broadly the climate crisis, are nothing more than the unexpected upheaval of the planetary ecosystem's responses. ... The Indigenous peoples of Siberia, among whom I have lived, always suspected that what we call \"natural disasters\" are actually responses from the mountain and the sky to human misbehavior. ... The crisis we are living through is cosmological, because just as we thought we were finally alone in an artificial world, the Earth responds to us with force.\nThis rupture with our nourishing environment is unprecedented in human history. While humans long lived in dense networks of interdependence with their environments, modernity has shifted us into more dispersed networks, giving us an illusion of total control and making us deaf to any response from the environment.\nHeatwaves, wildfires, and more broadly the climate crisis, are nothing more than the unexpected upheaval of the planetary ecosystem's responses. The Earth's reactions to the actions of modern societies are now on the same scale: colossal and uncontrollable. The Indigenous peoples of Siberia, among whom I have lived, always suspected that what we call \"natural disasters\" are actually responses from the mountain and the sky to human misbehavior. Soviet modernity tried to teach them the opposite: The mountain is there to be exploited, it is mute and does not respond. Yet, when Soviet modernity collapsed, the Indigenous peoples saw that the mountain was still there and that they depended on it.\nThe crisis we are living through is cosmological, because just as we thought we were finally alone in an artificial world, the Earth responds to us with force. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSustainability and the Astrobiological Perspective: Framing Human Futures in a Planetary Context (https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.3851)\nciteturn11academia23 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.9 years ago; We then cast the problem into the language of dynamical system theory and introduce the concept of a trajectory bundle for SWEIT evolution and discuss how astrobiological results usefully inform the creation of dynamical equations, their constraints and initial conditions.Three specific examples of how astrobiological considerations can be folded into discussions of sustainability are discussed: (1) concepts of planetary habitability, (2) mass extinctions and their possible relation to the current, so-called Anthropocene epoch, and (3) today's changes in atmospheric chemisty (and the climate change it entails) in the context of pervious epochs of biosphere-driven atmospheric and climate alteration (i.e. the Great Oxidation Event).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTriggering A Climate Change Dominated \"Anthropocene\": Is It Common Among Exocivilizations? (https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.06330)\nciteturn11academia24 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.5 years ago; Date: Wed Mar 10 20:25:28 2021We seek to model the coupled evolution of a planet and a civilization through the era when energy harvesting by the civilization drives the planet into new and adverse climate states. ... Our models show that the civilization's growth is truncated by planetary feedback (a \"climate dominated anthropocene\") for a significant region of the initial parameter space.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEarth as a Hybrid Planet - The Anthropocene in an Evolutionary Astrobiological Context (https://arxiv.org/abs/1708.08121)\nciteturn11academia25 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.1 years ago; Title: Earth as a Hybrid Planet - The Anthropocene in an Evolutionary Astrobiological Context ... In this perspective, the beginning of the Anthropocene can be seen as the onset of the hybridization of the planet - a transitional stage from one class of planetary systems interaction to another.\n"
